headline":"Flood Warning issued October 31 at 9:42AM MDT until November 2 at 11:00PM MST by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"...The National Weather Service in Grand Junction CO has issued a\nFlood Warning for the following rivers in Colorado...\n\nSan Juan River at Pagosa Springs affecting Archuleta County.\n\nFor the San Juan River...including Pagosa Springs...Major flooding is\nforecast.\n\nAdditional information is available at www.weather.gov/gjt.\n\nThe next statement will be issued Monday morning at 845 AM MST.\n\n* WHAT...Major flooding is forecast. This approaches the flood of\nrecord.\n\n* WHERE...San Juan River at Pagosa Springs.\n\n* WHEN...From Sunday afternoon to Monday evening.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 9:00 AM MDT Saturday the stage was 7.0 feet.\n- Bankfull stage is 8.5 feet.\n- Forecast...The river is expected to rise above flood stage\ntomorrow afternoon to a crest of 17.0 feet early Monday morning.\nIt will then fall below flood stage Monday afternoon.\n- Flood stage is 10.5 feet.\n- Flood History...This crest compares to a previous crest of 17.8\nfeet on 10/05/1911.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T23:00:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.be4b52948b839a0d5fe81ffdeffaa397353f7f12.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.be4b52948b839a0d5fe81ffdeffaa397353f7f12.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued October 30 at 3:02PM MDT until November 2 at 11:00AM MST by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall continues to be\npossible.\n\n* WHERE...Portions of southwest Colorado, including the following\nareas, Animas River Basin, Four Corners/Upper Dolores River, San\nJuan River Basin and Southwest San Juan Mountains and southeast\nUtah, including the following areas, Canyonlands/Natural Bridges,\nLa Sal and Abajo Mountains and Southeast Utah.\n\n* WHEN...From late Saturday night through Monday morning.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, recently burned areas, and other low-lying and\nflood-prone locations. Low-water crossings may be flooded. Storm\ndrains and ditches may become clogged with debris.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- 1 to 3 inches of rain is expected across portions of southeast\nUtah and southwest Colorado from Sunday morning through Monday\nmorning over soils that are still saturated from recent rainfall\nand flooding.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T11:00:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Southwest San Juan Mountains; Four Corners/Upper Dolores River; Animas River Basin; San Juan River Basin; Southeast Utah; La Sal and Abajo Mountains; Canyonlands/Natural Bridges","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.d9e2fd526e1e61f9532cd34833bc42a40d097b54.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.d9e2fd526e1e61f9532cd34833bc42a40d097b54.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-10-31T11:37:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KGJT 311137 AFDGJT Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Grand Junction CO 537 AM MDT Sat Oct 31 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Showers continue through Friday night. Lower chances of precipitation Saturday afternoon. - Flooding/flash flooding concerns increase Sunday into Monday, especially for areas that have saturated soils from last week's rain. A Flood Watch remains in effect.","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=AFD&format=CI"},"hwo":null,"fwf":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-10-31T06:36:00+00:00","snippet":".DISCUSSION... This is an automatically generated forecast with no discussion. The normal Fire Weather Forecast will resume in its entirety in the spring. ...GUSTY ERRATIC WINDS CAN BE EXPECTED NEAR ANY SHOWERS OR THUNDERSTORMS... ...THE SMOKE DATA IS CALCULATED FROM AN AVERAGE AT 6000 FEET MSL... COZ200-312200- Little Snake Forecast Area- 1235 AM MDT Sat Oct 31 